    The Admin UI search improvements (#7497)
    
    This partially addresses #7323.
    
    This patch attempts to address use cases 1 and 2 from the linked issue.
    It should allow users to search for partial strings again without
    disregarding the benefits of the new behaviour entirely.
    
    Reviewing the comments on related issues (#7323, #7324), I identified
    several issues with our implementation. The first of these is the `text`
    field. Opencast puts all searchable strings into a field named `text`.
    Only this field is analysed and indexed for the autocomplete Admin UI
    search. We then simply search this field for matches. The problem is
    that all strings in the `text` field are analysed and indexed in the
    same way. We cannot specify different settings for the `title` and
    `subject` fields, for example. More technical fields, such as room
    numbers or IDs, should be analysed differently too. Lastly, the fields
    that should be part of the `text` field are defined in the Opencast
    code. There is a better way to do this.
    
    OpenSearch introduces subfields for cases where different or multiple
    analyser/index settings could be defined per field. This patch makes use
    of them. I have dropped the `text` field and defined a `search` subfield
    for the searchable metadata fields. This allows us to configure each
    field individually and select the relevant fields in the schema itself.
    This change shouldn't affect the search results. However, the next one
    does.
    
    The whitespace tokenizer, introduced by #6498, works well with
    human-readable text. However, it is not effective with structural inline
    metadata, as described in use cases 1 and 2 of #7323. I have changed
    this so that text is split using whitespace, punctuation and symbols.
    This is done for fields that we currently index for search purposes. We
    should review the list of indexed fields at some point.
    
    Some curl commands to get more information about generated tokens.
    
    ```
    curl -X POST 'http://localhost:9200/opencast_series/_analyze?pretty' \
    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
    -d '
    {
      "field": "title.search",
      "text": "lti:123:Course - Informatik I",
      "explain": false
    }
    '
    ```
    
    The following subfields are generated too
    
    - search._2gram
    - search._3gram
    - search._index_prefix
    
    Especially the subfield `search._index_prefix`, generated by the
    `search_as_you_type` type definition is responsible for autocompletion
    search. Try also the `explain` parameter. Setting the value to `true`
    will show, how the result is created.
    
    ```
    curl -X POST 'http://localhost:9200/opencast_event/_search?pretty' \
    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
    -d '
    {
        "query": {
            "bool" : {
                "filter" : [ {
                    "terms" : {
                        "organization" : [ "mh_default_org" ],
                        "boost" : 1.0
                    }
                } ],
                "should" : [ {
                    "multi_match" : {
                        "query" : "Informatik",
                        "fields" : [ "*.search^1.2", "*.search._2gram^1.2", "*.search._3gram^1.2", "uid^1.0", "series_id^1.0" ],
                        "type" : "bool_prefix",
                        "operator" : "AND",
                        "slop" : 0,
                        "fuzziness" : "AUTO",
                        "prefix_length" : 0,
                        "max_expansions" : 50,
                        "zero_terms_query" : "NONE",
                        "auto_generate_synonyms_phrase_query" : true,
                        "fuzzy_transpositions" : false,
                        "boost" : 1.0
                    }
                } ],
                "adjust_pure_negative" : true,
                "minimum_should_match" : "1",
                "boost" : 1.0
            }
        }
    }
    '
    ```
